WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:03.700
Have you ever seen this sign in a store window
and thought…

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.390
“Can they do that?”

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:10.610
After all, it says right on the dollar bill:
“This note is legal tender for all debts,

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.219
public and private.”

00:00:12.219 --> 00:00:16.810
How can a business turn someone away who’s
carrying the same currency our country has

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.160
used for hundreds of years?

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.140
Are we becoming… a cashless society?

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.200
In an earlier episode, we talked about how
money is a global game of trust.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.440
The only reason a dollar bill has value is
because everyone has agreed to pretend that

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.440
it does.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.449
And in the last half-century, this game of
make-believe has evolved to a mind-bogglingly

00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:47.980
abstract level.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:54.079
Now, instead of hoarding paper slips and hunks
of metal as if they’re of any use whatsoever,

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:59.180
we walk up to a computer screen, tell it who
we are, it flashes some symbols at us, and

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.910
we walk away totally confident that we’ll
be able to exchange that digital information

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.510
for goods and services.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:07.360
Pretty weird when you think about it.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:13.330
Today it’s estimated that less than 10%
of the money in the world is cash, worth roughly

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.730
5 trillion in US dollars.

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:20.581
That includes all the stacks in all the bank
vaults, all the bills in your wallet, all

00:01:20.581 --> 00:01:23.370
the coins in all the couches in the world.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:28.360
The other 55 trillion or so exists only in
the minds of computers.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.370
The transition isn’t actually that surprising.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:35.810
After all, we moved from gold to paper because
paper is lighter and easier to carry.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:39.781
Ones and zeroes are even lighter than paper
and can travel at the speed of light, so if

00:01:39.781 --> 00:01:44.580
the object is to make currency as easy to
move around as possible, going digital is

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.680
just the logical next step in the evolution
of money.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.110
Which brings us back to the cashless store.

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:55.880
Operators of these establishments claim that
eliminating cash boosts productivity and efficiency.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:00.020
Employees no longer have to make change, count
bills or roll quarters.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.630
Food handlers don’t have to touch money
teeming with bacteria and viruses.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:07.840
And cashless registers present no incentive
for theft or robbery.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.720
So if going digital only makes stores faster,
cleaner and safer, why are an increasing number

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.340
of cities and states banning the practice?

00:02:15.359 --> 00:02:19.689
Many people believe that, as well-intentioned
as they may be, cashless stores amount to

00:02:19.689 --> 00:02:21.680
a form of discrimination.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:27.970
According to the FDIC, as of 2017, 6.5% of
Americans are “unbanked,” meaning they

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:31.160
have no checking or savings account, and no
credit cards.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.430
That’s 8.4 million households.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:38.930
And another 24 million are “underbanked,”
which means that though they may have an account,

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:44.310
they still rely on cash or money orders for
virtually all transactions.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:49.439
These people are disproportionately likely
to be poor, minorities, immigrants, or the

00:02:49.439 --> 00:02:50.439
elderly.

00:02:50.439 --> 00:02:54.879
Opponents of cashless stores claim that for
those in these groups, this sign may as well

00:02:54.879 --> 00:02:56.909
say, “You’re not welcome here.”

00:02:56.909 --> 00:02:59.280
But how can they do it, anyway?

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.640
If you want to run a business, don’t you
have to accept “legal tender”?

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.180
According to the Federal Reserve… no.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:09.720
Section 31 states
that “United States coins and currency…

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:13.740
are legal tender for all debts, public charges,
taxes, and dues.”

00:03:13.749 --> 00:03:18.760
This has been interpreted to mean that creditors
must accept cash for any debts owed to them,

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.920
but a business owner cannot be forced to accept
cash in exchange for goods and services.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.730
At least, not by the federal government.

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:29.730
Cities and states are free to make their own
regulations, and that’s exactly what they’ve

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:30.730
been doing.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:35.549
Philadelphia, San Francisco and New Jersey
have all banned cashless stores on the basis

00:03:35.549 --> 00:03:39.810
that it is discriminatory, and New York and
Rhode Island are currently considering similar

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.220
legislation.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:45.359
Some owners are calling these regulations
“burdensome,” (which is an odd way to

00:03:45.359 --> 00:03:50.790
describe receiving money from customers);
and they propose that instead of forcing businesses

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:56.889
to retain outdated practices, cities and states
should focus on making electronic transactions

00:03:56.889 --> 00:03:59.540
more attainable for underprivileged groups.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.989
Meanwhile, some businesses are already working
on innovations to satisfy everyone.

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:09.079
Amazon Go, for instance, has retrofitted its
automated checkout machines to accept cash,

00:04:09.079 --> 00:04:13.939
and the Mercedes-Benz Stadium in Atlanta has
kiosks where you can exchange cash for a kind

00:04:13.939 --> 00:04:16.940
of debit card you can then use to buy your
hot dogs and beer.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:21.230
The fact is, people still value cash for a
variety of reasons.

00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:23.380
For one thing, it’s anonymous.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:28.250
You don’t have to be drug-dealer or gun-runner
to be uncomfortable with the idea of corporations

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:31.500
and governments keeping track of every dime
you spend.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.040
And computers aren’t 100% reliable.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:40.100
In 2018, a hardware failure at Visa prevented
millions of cardholders across Europe from

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:42.480
making transactions for hours.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.540
And in the aftermath of Hurricane María in
Puerto Rico, cash was the only means of payment

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:48.540
available.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.900
Cash is also used by many people as a budgeting
tool.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:56.310
When Julia and I go grocery shopping, we leave
our credit cards at home and bring only as

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:00.060
much cash as we’ve budgeted, so we know
we won’t overspend.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.560
Studies have also shown that spending physical
currency activates the pain centers in the

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.910
brain in a way that using plastic doesn’t.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:12.900
This means that the more regularly you use
cash, the more frugal you’re likely to be.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:17.110
Credit card companies seem to be aware of
this fact, as they’ve been major proponents

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:23.350
of the cashless movement, with Visa even offering
prizes of $10,000 to small businesses that

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:26.730
pledge to stop accepting cash as a form of
payment.

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:30.750
Though this issue has gotten a lot of public
attention, the number of cashless stores in

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.930
the country is still very tiny.

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:34.680
Far more are cash only.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:40.950
30% of all American transactions are in cash,
including the majority of those under $10.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:47.610
There are almost 70 billion individual pieces
of physical U.S. currency in circulation,

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:50.500
and that number is going up, not down.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:55.550
Though many experts think a cashless society
is inevitable, it still seems to be a long

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:56.550
way off.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.870
Until then, we’ll keep bringing it to the
grocery store to shop for food.

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:03.470
Just remember to wash your hands before you
make dinner.

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:04.780
And that’s our two cents!

